A||||11||AkursedX||Changing From F.I. to a carb||||5||
Z||000000||AkursedX||11-27-2001||09:32 PM||||I'm curious, what would be involved in swapping a 2.8 (well actually, it would be a 3.1) fuel injection system for a carb system instead?<P>I know you would replace the whole intake and all that stuff, but what I want to know is the small stuff.  Like what computer stuff gets removed, how the throttle cables get run and any other small stuff.<P>I think there are a couple of you guys out there who have done this.  If you could give me some info on what it takes, it would be greatly appreciated.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||GTDude||11-27-2001||09:37 PM||||WHY? You will loose drivability, horsepower!  I can think of no reason to make the switch.  From carb to f.i. sure......but not the other way around.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fix||11-27-2001||09:50 PM||||Haven't done this myself, but I don't think it would be that hard.  I agree that it's probably not a wise swap, but I'll throw in my 2 cents anyway.  You'll need a new intake obviously, and after that the only tricky part I can think of is regulating fuel pressure.  The V6 fuel pumps put out too much pressure to use a fuel pressure regulator and will burn up pretty quickly, so you'll have to swap it out for a 4 cylinder fuel pump.  I think it requires a little moddifying, but not much.  Anyway, the 4 cylinder pump can be regulated down to the appropriate pressure of 8psi or so.  Hope that helps||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||11-27-2001||09:54 PM||||It's easier to run huge cams and high compression with carbs.  <P>I'm not sure about specifics, but I'm pretty sure you can take off all the sensors except for the gauge ones (coolant temp, oil pressure, etc).  Need to get a distributor with mechanical advance, fuel pressure regulator (need 10-14 psi instead of the 40+psi)  <P>As much as I like fuel injection, one of the many projects I want to do one day is build up a fire-breathing 60* v6 with a huuuuuge cam and carb it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||AkursedX||11-27-2001||10:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GTDude:<BR><B>WHY? You will loose drivability, horsepower!  I can think of no reason to make the switch.  From carb to f.i. sure......but not the other way around.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, I am just brainstorming right now.<P>I know I want a high performance engine, and the only thing I have decided on is a rebuilt 3.1 block for my foundation (Don't bother lecturing me on all the other swaps, I know more than enough about all the other swaps, this is my personal choice).  <P>So, when you get into heavy modification, the thing that becomes the weakest part is the intake.  The Fiero intake is very restrictive and your performance is hindered by the amount of air you can flow more than anything else.<P>I could turbo, I could supercharge, but those cost big bucks, and I don't have the connections to a fabricator or a chip burner to make my own kit.<P>So, this brings me to the idea of a carb.  Edelbrock makes and intake and the carb so finding a source would be easier, and I am assuming it is cheaper than a turbo kit. So I figured I might as well ask.<P>Unless west coast fiero makes their dual TB kit soon, a carb seems like the best option to me.<P>There is a guy on the board who makes 270hp out of a 3.2 with a carb, so I know I will be able to get some big power if I do it like that.  (Although my gas mileage would go right to crap  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) <P><BR><P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||11-27-2001||11:16 PM||||Ive wondered how carb would do on my turboed engine. It would cut out lots of FI problems completely. I think you have to also change out the distributor, so you can throw away your ecm too. The fuel pump is no prob, just get an aftermarket electric pump from like Jegs for $30. Then you never even have to take out tank to fix a pump.||||reg||1
Z||000006||MR2KLR||11-28-2001||07:00 AM||||You will need to se a S-10 Distributor. (mec. advance) new coil, Intake manifold and carb. I also know that you will need a late model intake, If you get one designed for older cast heads it won't work with a 3.1's alu heads.<P>I think a carb would be better than the 2.8's FI you can simply flow better with a 500-750 CFM. carb, a big cam and headers. and if you get one with electric choke and just give her a min. to warm up I see no driveability probs.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://members.tripod.com/wawoonigootphet/images/af/3in1_vu1.jpg">  <A HREF="http://community-2.webtv.net/TOMTOM81/MYNEWTOY/" TARGET=_blank>Tom's Page</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||falconhulk||11-28-2001||08:19 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>I think a carb would be better than the 2.8's FI you can simply flow better with a 500-750 CFM. carb, a big cam and headers. and if you get one with electric choke and just give her a min. to warm up I see no driveability probs.[QUOTE]<P>750 cfm????  Not on that size engine.  Car would run like crap.  Thats the size carb for a 5.0 or higher and then only with vacum secondaries!<P>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-28-2001||10:18 AM||||I have a 2.8 carbed motor in mine and I like it.   I hated the problems I had with my 4cyl fi so I was happy to toss everything when I swapped motors  (I I used a buick 2.8 carbed motor) <P>Fuel pump pumping out too much gas is no problem since there is a fuel return line and you can lower your fuel pressure.<P>My existing gas cable was modified a little to attach to the carb and and like previously posted, you would need another distributer. <P>Computer stays hooked up (for the rest of the car) and the sensors are unplugged & removed.<P><BR>Would you have any issue with emissions in CA since the car origionally came FI?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||AkursedX||11-28-2001||11:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by isthiswhereiputausername?:<BR><B><P>My existing gas cable was modified a little to attach to the carb and and like previously posted, you would need another distributer. <P>Computer stays hooked up (for the rest of the car) and the sensors are unplugged & removed.<P><BR>Would you have any issue with emissions in CA since the car origionally came FI?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Do you have any pics of your setup by any chance?<P>This is starting to look pretty feasable, the whole edelbrock intake and carb setup is pretty cheap, and it doesn't seem like too much custom work.<P>I live in NY so I won't have to worry about CA emissions, so I think I can do it legally.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-28-2001||12:26 PM||||I have none right now but my next day off, I will get a few done up.  theres another forum member interested in doing up a carbed motor also and I told him I will send him pics also<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||loafer87gt||11-28-2001||02:40 PM||||A guy on the list named Tajiguy did a carb swap on his ride. He even posted some sound clips of it revved out.||||reg||1
